From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Waltteri Hooli <wahooli@localhost>
Date: Wed, 10 Sep 2026 15:30:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 75/75] local: drm/msm/a6xx: do not claim the RSC slept after a
 failed power-off

a6xx_rpmh_stop() polls RSCC_RSC_STATUS0_DRV0 for the sleep acknowledgement,
logs "Unable to power off the GPU RSC" when it times out -- and then sets
GMU_STATUS_PDC_SLEEP anyway, on both paths. The bit's contract is "this
kernel put the RSC to sleep", which on the error path is exactly what did
not happen.

The next bring-up then reads the cold/warm state from hardware, finds a warm
GMU, so patch 0068's cold-boot clear does not apply, and a6xx_rpmh_start()
asks the RSC to wake from a sleep it never entered. The ACK cannot arrive.
Observed on x1e80100 at EL2 roughly three minutes after every cross-boot
hibernate resume, and it takes the GPU out for good:

  [ 939.611] platform 3d6a000.gmu: GMU cold boot (TCM_RET_CNTL=0x0)
  [ 940.947] *ERROR* Unable to power off the GPU RSC
  [1119.667] platform 3d6a000.gmu: GMU warm boot (TCM_RET_CNTL=0x1)
  [1119.678] *ERROR* Unable to power on the GPU RSC
  [1121.732] *ERROR* Timeout waiting for GMU OOB set GPU_SET: 0x0   (x2)
  [1132.091] msm_dpu: 43050c01: preemption timed out
  [1132.091] *ERROR* 43050c01: hangcheck recover!

leaving 3d00000.gpu at runtime_status=error. The first bring-up after the
resume is the cold one and it succeeds -- 0068 handles that -- so this is a
distinct bug from 0068, one step later in the same sequence, and it needs a
use/idle/use cycle to show up.

Only set the bit when the acknowledgement actually arrived, and say so when
it did not, so the next bring-up's behaviour is attributable.

RESIDUAL RISK, stated because it is not zero: if the RSC really did enter
sleep and only the status poll failed, skipping the wake leaves it asleep and
the GPU will not power up. That failure is loud and looks exactly like the
one this replaces, so a run can tell them apart. The observed evidence points
the other way -- the bring-up that follows the failed power-off reports a
*warm* GMU, i.e. the RSC was not in the state the bit claimed.

Depends on 0068 (both touch a6xx_gmu.c; 0068 clears the same bit on a cold
boot).

Signed-off-by: Waltteri Hooli <wahooli@localhost>
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/msm/adreno/a6xx_gmu.c | 13 ++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/adreno/a6xx_gmu.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/adreno/a6xx_gmu.c
index c8bc477ab..e0281c67c 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/adreno/a6xx_gmu.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/adreno/a6xx_gmu.c
@@ -657,7 +657,18 @@ static void a6xx_rpmh_stop(struct a6xx_gmu *gmu)
 
 	gmu_write(gmu, REG_A6XX_GMU_RSCC_CONTROL_REQ, 0);
 
-	set_bit(GMU_STATUS_PDC_SLEEP, &gmu->status);
+	/*
+	 * GMU_STATUS_PDC_SLEEP means "this kernel put the RSC to sleep", and
+	 * a6xx_rpmh_start() waits for a wake ACK on the strength of it. If the
+	 * sleep was never acknowledged we do not know that it happened, and
+	 * claiming it makes the next warm bring-up hang on an ACK that cannot
+	 * arrive.
+	 */
+	if (!ret)
+		set_bit(GMU_STATUS_PDC_SLEEP, &gmu->status);
+	else
+		dev_warn(gmu->dev,
+			 "RSC sleep not acknowledged, not arming the wake\n");
 }
 
 static inline void pdc_write(void __iomem *ptr, u32 offset, u32 value)
